Q1. What is a Variable?

A variable is a named location in memory used to store a value.

name = "Netaji"
age = 32
salary = 75000.50

Q2. What is a Data Type?
A data type defines:

What kind of value a variable can hold.
What operations can be performed on that value.

Q3. What is the difference between type() and isinstance()?
type(obj): returns the exact type of an object.
isinstance(obj, Type): checks whether an object is an instance of a type or its subclasses, making it more flexible and the preferred choice in most code.

Q4. Is Python statically typed or dynamically typed?

Python is dynamically typed, meaning you don't declare variable types explicitly, and a variable can reference objects of different types during execution.

Note: Python Tip (Very Important)

Unlike C#, Python has only a handful of fundamental data types, and everything is an object, including integers, strings, and booleans. This consistent object model is one of the reasons Python feels simple yet powerful.

Q5. What is an Operator?

An operator is a symbol that performs an operation on one or more operands.

Q6. Identity Operators

These are unique to Python and are frequently asked in interviews.

is: Checks whether two variables refer to the same object.

Q7. What is the difference between == and is?
==: Compares values
is: Compares object identity (same object in memory)

Q8. what is Membership Operators in Python?

These check whether a value exists in a sequence.

Notes on list: Key Takeaways
Lists store multiple values.
Lists are ordered.
Lists are mutable.
Indexing starts at 0.
Negative indexes access items from the end.
append() adds one item.
extend() adds multiple items.
remove() removes by value.
pop() removes by index.
sort() modifies the original list.
sorted() returns a new sorted list.
copy() prevents accidental reference sharing.
List comprehensions provide concise transformations and filtering.
